B.eS=new A.yw(0,"standards")
B.bNA=new A.jG("IEEE / 802.11 Working Group","IEEE",B.eS,"Standards development",'The actual Wi-Fi standard. "IEEE Std 802.11, Part 11: Wireless LAN MAC and PHY Specifications" and every amendment (11n / ac / ax / be). The IEEE Standards Association ratifies and publishes it.','This is where Wi-Fi is genuinely defined. 802.11be is Wi-Fi 7, 802.11ax is Wi-Fi 6. The amendment letter is the real name; the "Wi-Fi 7" label is Wi-Fi Alliance marketing. Current generation = Wi-Fi 7 (802.11be) as of 2026-06-09; Wi-Fi 8 (802.11bn) is in development.',"https://www.ieee802.org/11/",!1)
B.bNz=new A.jG(u.G,"ETSI",B.eS,"Standards development (EU-recognized SDO)","Harmonised European Standards (EN 300 328 for 2.4 GHz, EN 301 893 for 5 GHz, EN 303 687 for 6 GHz) that underpin RED / CE compliance. A 3GPP Organizational Partner.","Dual role. Here it is a standards developer. It is also the EU's referenced harmonizer: comply with the EN and you get presumption of conformity for CE marking. ETSI shapes EU radio rules but is NOT the legal regulator; the national authority is. See Regulatory Domains for the legal per-country rules.","https://www.etsi.org",!1)
B.bNo=new A.jG("3rd Generation Partnership Project","3GPP",B.eS,"Standards development (cellular)","The cellular standards: GSM / UMTS / LTE / 5G NR / 5G-Advanced. A partnership of seven SDOs (ARIB, ATIS, CCSA, ETSI, TSDSI, TTA, TTC).","Context and convergence. 3GPP specs define non-3GPP-access interworking: how Wi-Fi offload, Wi-Fi / cellular handoff, and OpenRoaming's cellular side actually work.","https://www.3gpp.org",!1)
B.bNp=new A.jG("Internet Engineering Task Force","IETF",B.eS,"Standards development (open, no formal membership)",'The IP / transport / application protocols Wi-Fi carries: IP, TCP, UDP, QUIC, TLS, DNS, DHCP, RADIUS - published as RFCs. Runs on "rough consensus and running code."',"Wi-Fi is just the link layer. Everything above it - addressing, DHCP, TLS, RADIUS auth for 802.1X enterprise Wi-Fi, captive portals, Passpoint auth - rides IETF protocols.","https://www.ietf.org",!1)
B.bNw=new A.jG("Bluetooth Special Interest Group","Bluetooth SIG",B.eS,"Standards development + certification + trade association","The Bluetooth core specification, the Bluetooth qualification (certification) program, and the Bluetooth trademark licensing.","Adjacent PAN tech sharing the 2.4 GHz band - a coexistence and interference factor on every Wi-Fi deployment. Also an instructive parallel: one body that does BOTH the standard and the cert, unlike Wi-Fi's IEEE / Wi-Fi Alliance split.","https://www.bluetooth.com",!1)
B.bNu=new A.jG("Ecma International","Ecma",B.eS,"Standards development (general ICT)","ECMAScript (JavaScript), Office Open XML, Dart - and, relevant here, NFC standards (with ETSI / ISO) and close-proximity data-transfer specs.","Context only - adjacent, NOT a Wi-Fi body. NFC is its single wireless touchpoint. Shown to round out the ICT-standards landscape.","https://ecma-international.org",!0)
B.jo=new A.yw(1,"certification")
B.bNq=new A.jG("Wi-Fi Alliance","WFA",B.jo,"Certification + industry advocacy + branding",'The Wi-Fi CERTIFIED interoperability program; the "Wi-Fi" trademark; the consumer generation names (Wi-Fi 4 / 5 / 6 / 6E / 7); programs like WPA3, Passpoint, and EasyMesh.','Owns the brand and the interop seal - it does NOT write 802.11. The Wi-Fi 6 / 7 names you sell to clients come from here, not from IEEE. "Wi-Fi" is a Wi-Fi Alliance trademark, not an acronym, and does NOT stand for "Wireless Fidelity."',"https://www.wi-fi.org",!1)
B.bNx=new A.jG("Wireless Broadband Alliance","WBA",B.jo,"Industry advocacy + guidelines + certification","OpenRoaming (the global secure Wi-Fi roaming federation) and the WRIX roaming-exchange standards; Wi-Fi industry trials, guidelines, and advocacy. Founded 2003.",'OpenRoaming is the "connect once, roam everywhere" framework bridging Wi-Fi and cellular identities. The body driving carrier-grade public Wi-Fi.',"https://wballiance.com",!1)
B.bNy=new A.jG("CWNP (Certified Wireless Network Professional)","CWNP",B.jo,"Professional certification (vendor-neutral)","The vendor-neutral enterprise Wi-Fi certification track: CWNA, then CWAP / CWDP / CWSP / CWNT, up to CWNE (plus newer Wireless IoT credentials). Tracks as of 2026; CWNP periodically revises them. Founded 1999.","The credential that validates real 802.11 skill across any vendor's gear. The professional, human-skills layer beneath all the org acronyms - it certifies people, not products.","https://www.cwnp.com",!1)
B.oU=new A.yw(2,"spectrum")
B.bNv=new A.jG("International Telecommunication Union, Radiocommunication Sector","ITU-R",B.oU,"Global spectrum coordination (UN agency sector)","Global radio-spectrum allocation: the Radio Regulations, the World Radiocommunication Conferences (WRC), and the IMT framework (IMT-2020 = 5G, IMT-2030 = 6G). Maintains the Master International Frequency Register.","Sits above every national regulator. WRC decisions - for example whether 6 GHz goes to Wi-Fi or to IMT / 5G - cascade into the FCC, Ofcom, and ETSI-aligned rules. See Regulatory Domains for how those land per country.","https://www.itu.int/en/ITU-R/",!1)
B.jp=new A.yw(3,"iotAdjacent")
B.bNt=new A.jG("Wi-SUN Alliance","Wi-SUN",B.jp,"Industry alliance + certification","The Wi-SUN FAN (Field Area Network) specification and its certification program - an IPv6 sub-GHz wireless mesh built on IEEE 802.15.4g (PHY) / 802.15.4e (MAC), the IEEE 802.15.4-SUN family. Current spec FAN 1.1. Also issues cybersecurity certificates.","The dominant standard for utility-scale outdoor IoT mesh - smart metering (AMI), distribution automation, smart streetlighting, smart cities. The sub-GHz, long-range, many-hop counterpart to the Wi-Fi you design indoors.","https://wi-sun.org",!1)
B.bNs=new A.jG("Connectivity Standards Alliance","CSA",B.jp,"Standards development + certification","Owns and maintains BOTH Zigbee (its founding 802.15.4-based mesh) AND Matter (the IP-based, royalty-free smart-home interoperability standard, backed by Apple / Google / Amazon / Samsung). Runs the certification programs for both. Formerly the Zigbee Alliance, renamed 11 May 2021.","The body behind most smart-home radios your clients already run. Matter rides IP over Wi-Fi and Thread, so it lands directly on the WLAN - Matter-over-Wi-Fi traffic is on your network whether you planned for it or not.","https://csa-iot.org",!1)
B.bNr=new A.jG("LoRa Alliance","LoRa Alliance",B.jp,"Industry alliance + certification","Develops, maintains, and certifies the LoRaWAN standard - the open LPWAN MAC-layer / network protocol. LoRaWAN runs OVER the LoRa physical layer, which is the proprietary spread-spectrum PHY owned by Semtech (Semtech is a member, not the standards body).","The carrier-grade, kilometers-range, battery-decade LPWAN for sensors - a different problem than Wi-Fi solves, often deployed alongside it. Know the split: LoRa = Semtech's PHY / chip; LoRaWAN = the LoRa Alliance's open standard.","https://lora-alliance.org",!1)
B.r_=s([B.bNA,B.bNz,B.bNo,B.bNp,B.bNw,B.bNu,B.bNq,B.bNx,B.bNy,B.bNv,B.bNt,B.bNs,B.bNr],A.M("q<jG>"))
B.B6=s(["Layer 2, single hop. Switches do not forward LLDP or CDP frames. You only ever see the device on the other end of your own cable, never anything past it.","Wired only. These frames do not cross Wi-Fi. A laptop on Wi-Fi sees nothing; you must be on the wired NIC (on a Mac, usually a USB or Thunderbolt Ethernet adapter).","The switch has to have it turned on. Cisco IOS runs CDP by default but ships LLDP off; many non-Cisco switches also ship LLDP off. When nothing shows up, first check whether the switch is sending, not whether your tool is broken."],t.s)
