B.bug=new A.B2(B.u5,"2.4 GHz","2400 - 2483.5 MHz",B.kX,"83.5 MHz (US)","802.11b / g / n / ax (Wi-Fi 4 / 6)","Ch 1-11 (US) \xb7 Ch 1-13 (EU) \xb7 Ch 1-14 (JP)","3 channels at 20 MHz: 1, 6, 11","20 MHz, 40 MHz","None","Bluetooth (2.402-2.480 GHz), Zigbee (2.4 GHz), microwave ovens (~2.45 GHz), baby monitors, cordless phones","High interference environment. Best wall penetration of the three bands. Avoid as primary band in dense enterprise deployments. Wi-Fi 5 (802.11ac) does not operate in 2.4 GHz.")
B.buh=new A.B2(B.Qm,"5 GHz","5150 - 5850 MHz (US UNII-1/2A/2C/3)",B.cA,"~580 MHz usable (US UNII-1/2A/2C/3)","802.11a / n / ac / ax (Wi-Fi 5 / 6)","25 channels at 20 MHz (US, including DFS)","25 @ 20 MHz \xb7 12 @ 40 MHz \xb7 6 @ 80 MHz \xb7 2 @ 160 MHz","20 / 40 / 80 / 160 MHz","Required in UNII-2A (5250-5350 MHz) and UNII-2C (5470-5725 MHz) - radar avoidance mandatory","Weather radar, terminal Doppler radar, military radar (all managed by DFS)","UNII-1 (5150-5250 MHz): indoor use only in some regions. UNII-2A/2C require DFS - expect 60-second channel availability delay after radar detection. UNII-3 (5725-5850 MHz): no DFS.")
B.bui=new A.B2(B.Qn,"6 GHz","5925 - 7125 MHz",B.kY,"1200 MHz","802.11ax / be (Wi-Fi 6E / 7)","59 \xd7 20 MHz \xb7 29 \xd7 40 MHz \xb7 14 \xd7 80 MHz \xb7 7 \xd7 160 MHz \xb7 3 \xd7 320 MHz (Wi-Fi 7)","59 non-overlapping at 20 MHz \xb7 14 PSC (Preferred Scanning Channels) for APs","20 / 40 / 80 / 160 MHz (Wi-Fi 6E) \xb7 + 320 MHz (Wi-Fi 7)","No DFS - incumbent protection via AFC (Automated Frequency Coordination) for standard-power outdoor","Fixed microwave backhaul links (managed via AFC database)","Three power modes in US: Standard Power (up to 36 dBm EIRP, requires AFC outdoors) \xb7 Low Power Indoor / LPI (up to 30 dBm, no AFC) \xb7 Very Low Power / VLP (up to 14 dBm EIRP, no AFC, mobile use). No legacy Wi-Fi 4 or older devices - WPA3 mandatory.")
B.Cv=s([B.bug,B.buh,B.bui],A.M("q<B2>"))
B.bg2=new A.bP("Roaming is the hard problem. Clinician handsets, mobile carts, and telemetry move constantly and must roam facility-wide with no dead zones, including stairwells, elevators, and back-of-house where a code-alarm device still has to work. That means overlapping cells, fast-roaming support (802.11r, k, v), and voice-grade RF planning.",null)
B.bgr=new A.bP("Coverage grade drives AP count more than floor area does. The industry designs to three grades, each needing progressively more APs: data grade, then voice grade, then voice-and-RTLS grade. A hospital quoted at data-grade density will fail when it has to carry voice handsets and location services. Under-scoping here is one of the most common and expensive mistakes.",null)
B.bg7=new A.bP("RTLS (real-time location of assets, staff, and patients) is one of the highest-value hospital wireless applications, and it often has to run on the same infrastructure you are already building. The landscape:",null)
B.bhe=new A.is(B.Cr,null)
B.bgq=new A.bP("Supporting RTLS is what pushes the design to the voice-and-RTLS grade in the first place.",null)
B.bgZ=new A.bP("Segmentation is a performance control here, not only a security one. A ward full of guests streaming video must not share airtime with voice handsets, mobile chart sessions, or telemetry. Guest, clinical, device, and RTLS traffic get separated for airtime protection as much as for HIPAA.",null)
B.aOL=s([B.bg2,B.j,B.bgr,B.j,B.bg7,B.k,B.bhe,B.j,B.bgq,B.j,B.bgZ],t.p)
