B.bRW=new A.apZ("Read the spec, don\u2019t derive it.","\u201cMore gain means a narrower beam\u201d is always directionally true. The exact beamwidth that comes with a given gain depends on the specific antenna\u2019s design, so it is a rule of thumb, not a formula you can run in your head. Trust the published beamwidth on the antenna\u2019s data sheet. Don\u2019t try to back it out of the gain number.")
B.bRX=new A.apZ("Point-to-point needs real line-of-sight, not just a visible path.","The radio beam needs clearance around the straight line between the two antennas, the Fresnel zone, not only an unobstructed view. A link that looks clear to the eye can still be choked by a rooftop or a tree line sitting inside that zone.")
B.bS2=new A.aq1(null)
B.bS9=new A.aqk(null)
B.bSc=new A.xz("Practical prep: clean the CAD layers before import. Strip furniture, dimensions, and title blocks; keep walls, doors, and structure. Knowing the layer scheme (structured codes like A-WALL, A-DOOR, E-LITE) lets you tell the architect exactly which layers to send instead of receiving an unusable 80-layer dump.",null)
B.bSg=new A.xA("An RCP is drawn as if a mirror on the floor reflects the ceiling upward, so you see the ceiling in the same left-right orientation as the floor plan below it. It shows the ceiling grid (the T-bar layout, usually 2x2 or 2x4 ft tiles), mounting heights and ceiling elevations, and every ceiling-mounted element: light fixtures, HVAC diffusers and returns, sprinkler heads, speakers, exit signs, access panels, soffits, and bulkheads.",null)
B.bSh=new A.uJ("NEC Article 505 (gases) and Article 506 (dusts) adopt the IEC Zone system used worldwide under ATEX and IECEx. It splits the same risk into three bands.",null)
B.bSj=new A.Bz("A NEMA type maps to a minimum IP equivalent. The reverse is not valid. NEMA tests corrosion, icing, gasket aging, and oil, so a NEMA 4X enclosure exceeds IP66, but an IP66 enclosure is not automatically NEMA 4X. It was never corrosion-tested.",null)
B.bSo=new A.By("Some hazards mean stop work and call it in. Recognize them and hand them off. Do not improvise a workaround.",null)
B.bSp=new A.xA("A sheet number is a discipline letter, a sheet-type digit, and a sequence number.",null)
B.bSq=new A.xz("Why it matters: an LOD 100 to 200 model is a massing study, so do not derive wall attenuation or mounting detail from it. LOD 300 and up is dimensionally trustworthy. LOI (Level of Information) is the data-completeness sibling of LOD (geometry versus data).",null)
B.bSr=new A.Bz("NEMA types describe the same idea for US spec sheets, and they test things IP never checks: corrosion, external icing, gasket aging, and oil. The four types that matter for Wi-Fi:",null)
B.bSs=new A.IA("Engaging at SD is the difference between designing Wi-Fi in and retrofitting it later.",null)
B.bSu=new A.uJ("To put wireless in a classified area you need a recognized protection concept matched to the Division or Zone:",null)
B.bSv=new A.xz("The major Wi-Fi design tools (Ekahau, Hamina, iBwave) follow the same pattern:",null)
B.bSw=new A.xA("Telecom (T) is its own discipline. Structured cabling and often the WLAN live on the T sheets, and that is where your drawings coordinate.",null)
B.bSx=new A.uJ("Division to Zone, the mapping pros need:",null)
B.bSA=new A.Bz('An IP rating reads left to right: IP then two digits. First digit is solids and dust. Second digit is water. Each digit maps to a defined lab test, which is why the code is trustworthy and "waterproof" is not.',null)
B.bSB=new A.By('Four items are the common "let me on the site" baseline: hard hat, safety-toe footwear, high-visibility apparel, and eye protection. On a finished office install, street clothes are usually fine. On active construction, expect the GC to require the four to badge on.',null)
B.bSC=new A.uK("T568A and T568B are the two pin-out standards. A jack is wired to one, so be consistent end to end.",null)
B.bSQ=new A.uJ("A standard commercial AP has internal energy (radios, PoE, capacitors, switching) fully capable of igniting a flammable atmosphere on a fault, or even in normal operation.",null)
B.bTm=new A.QE(B.Az,null)
B.bTn=new A.QE(B.Bm,null)
B.bTo=new A.a3z(B.BR,null)
B.bTr=new A.a3A(B.Co,null)
B.bTu=new A.ar_(null)
B.bTv=new A.ar0(0,"warning")
B.RY=new A.ar0(1,"info")
B.bTw=new A.QI("Both systems are legal in the US, but you cannot mix Division and Zone classification in the same installation. Which one is in force is set by the facility's area-classification drawing and the AHJ, not by the installer.",null)
B.bTx=new A.QJ("Never treat any of these as a procedure to run yourself.",null)
B.bTy=new A.QJ("Confirm the site's specific PPE policy. The employer and GC set what is required, not the Toolbox.",null)
B.bTz=new A.a3G("Plus others (H, V, B, Q, D, R, X, Z, O). A second letter subdivides on big jobs (TN = Telecom / Data Networks, TY = Telecom / Security, EP = Electrical Power).",null)
B.bTA=new A.QI("US listing runs to UL 1203 for the explosionproof / Division method under NEC 500, and to the UL 60079 series for the Zone system under NEC 505 and 506. Internationally, ATEX (mandatory in Europe) and IECEx (global) apply.",null)
B.bTB=new A.IE("The full type list also includes 1, 2, 3, 3S, 5, 12, and 13 (indoor and specialized).",null)
B.bTC=new A.IE("NEMA publishes no exact conversion, and reputable charts disagree on a couple of rows. Where they differ, both values are shown. Treat these as approximate floors, never as certified equivalences.",null)
B.bTD=new A.QI("Div 2 is a far larger and more common market for rated wireless than Div 1.",null)
B.bTE=new A.IE("Two levels get misread. 8 is manufacturer-defined, not a fixed depth. 9K is about pressure-washing, a different hazard than immersion.",null)
B.bTF=new A.IE("The object-size ladder ends at 4 (1 mm). Levels 5 and 6 switch to a dust-chamber test, so they are about dust, not a bigger object.",null)
B.bTG=new A.QJ("The hard-hat Class G (2,200 V) and Class E (20,000 V) figures are dielectric proof-test voltages, not safe-working ratings; never treat them as a safe working limit.",null)
B.bTH=new A.a3G("So A-1xx is an architectural plan and E-6xx an electrical schedule.",null)
B.c3i=new A.bru(0,"elevated")
B.bSD=new A.uJ("Misreading a classified area gets someone hurt and gets you sued. Knowing Class, Division, and Zone lets you recognize the site, quote it honestly, and route the install to the right rated gear and the AHJ instead of walking into a job you cannot legally do.",null)
B.bTI=new A.rk("Why a WLAN pro cares",B.bSD,null)
B.bSE=new A.Iz("The six code articles that actually bite a WLAN installer. Each one is here so you can recognize it on site, then hand it to the AHJ, a licensed electrician, or the equipment listing. This is recognize-and-defer, never how-to-comply.",null)
B.bTJ=new A.QK(null,B.bSE,null)
B.bSM=new A.By("Half your installs happen on a ladder or lift, on an active site, around other trades. Knowing the PPE ratings a GC will ask for keeps you from being turned away at the gate, and a few dollars of ESD gear keeps a switch or optic from a latent failure you will get blamed for. The stop-and-call-it-in flags keep a drill bit from turning a routine cable pull into a health or legal incident.",null)
B.bTK=new A.BA("Why a WLAN pro cares",B.bSM,null)
B.bSK=new A.xz("What the building files an architect hands you actually are, and how they flow into a Wi-Fi design tool. The Toolbox explains these formats. It does not open, render, or convert them. That is a different and much larger product category, and this reference stays on the explaining side of that line.",null)
B.bTL=new A.xC(null,B.bSK,null)
B.bSe=new A.QC("The credential you do not have is the schedule you cannot keep. Rail screening, an ICRA permit, or lift-operator proof can each add days or weeks between winning the job and touching a cable. Scope these before you quote so the timeline you promise is the timeline you can hit.",null)
B.bTM=new A.IF("Why a WLAN pro cares",B.bSe,null)
B.bSP=new A.uK("The path runs MDF (main equipment room) to backbone and riser cabling to IDF (telecom room per floor or zone) to horizontal cabling to the work-area outlet or AP. AP count and placement drive IDF count and PoE-switch port budgeting, and the 90 m rule ties AP locations to IDF locations. This is where the WLAN design and the cabling design have to talk to each other.",null)
B.bTN=new A.uP("Topology and rooms",B.bSP,null)
B.bSi=new A.xz("The Toolbox explains DWG, IFC, RVT, and the rest, and how they reach Ekahau, Hamina, and iBwave. It is not a CAD or BIM viewer, converter, or editor.",null)
B.bTO=new A.xC("The boundary, stated plainly",B.bSi,null)
B.bSJ=new A.xA("How to read an architectural drawing set, so you can find the sheet you need, speak the same shorthand as the other trades, and put your APs on the one sheet that actually shows whether they fit. US convention (the National CAD Standard); sheet-numbering practice varies on smaller jobs.",null)
B.bTP=new A.xD(null,B.bSJ,null)
B.bSn=new A.xz("You receive these files on every design job, and the difference between a clean import and a wasted afternoon is knowing what the format is, what LOD you were handed, which layers to ask for, and that scale calibration is the step that makes or breaks the whole design.",null)
B.bTQ=new A.xC("Why a WLAN pro cares",B.bSn,null)
B.bSm=new A.uK("BICSI is the professional body for the cabling trade. Its flagship credential is the RCDD (Registered Communications Distribution Designer), and the TDMM (Telecommunications Distribution Methods Manual) is the RCDD's core reference. Knowing who the RCDD is on a job tells you who owns the cabling design you must coordinate with.",null)
B.bTR=new A.uP("BICSI",B.bSm,null)
B.bSy=new A.IA("The RFI, the submittal, the punch list, and the OAC are the levers that get your coverage design built the way you drew it. Knowing when to raise an RFI (the moment the RCP and your AP plan disagree) and how to get your gear approved as a submittal is the difference between a design that survives construction and one that gets value-engineered away while you were not in the room.",null)
B.bTS=new A.BC("Why a WLAN pro cares",B.bSy,null)
B.bSd=new A.QC("The pattern across every item: the requirement is set by someone other than you (the general contractor, site owner, rail or transit authority, or accreditation body), it must be satisfied before work starts, and it carries real lead time and cost. Confirm each one before you quote and before you mobilize.",null)
B.bTT=new A.IF(null,B.bSd,null)
B.bSt=new A.Iz("These six are the code articles most likely to surface on an everyday install: the hoistway you cannot use, the plenum that dictates your cable jacket, the PoE bundle that overheats, the antenna that needs grounding, the fire wall you must not breach, and the dead cable you must pull. Recognize each one, then defer to the right authority.",null)
B.bTU=new A.QK("Why a WLAN pro cares",B.bSt,null)
B.bSG=new A.QC("A pre-mobilization checklist. On many sites you cannot even reach the work area without a specific credential, background check, orientation, or escort. That is a quoting and scheduling factor, not just a safety one. Underestimating it is how a one-day install becomes a three-week mobilization.",null)
B.bTV=new A.IF(null,B.bSG,null)
B.bSk=new A.By("The personal protective equipment a general contractor expects before it badges you onto an active site, plus a short note on protecting the electronics you carry. This is awareness and lookup, not compliance instruction. The site and your employer own the PPE assessment.",null)
B.bTW=new A.BA(null,B.bSk,null)
B.bSH=new A.xA('US architectural sheets use fractional-inch scales: 1/8" = 1\'-0" (1:96), 1/4" = 1\'-0" (1:48), 1/2" = 1\'-0" (1:24), 1" = 1\'-0" (1:12). Site and civil sheets use the engineer\'s decimal scale (1" = 20\', 1" = 50\', 1" = 100\'). To get the ratio, invert the fraction and multiply by 12: 1/8" gives 8 x 12 = 1:96.',null)
B.bTX=new A.xD("Scales",B.bSH,null)
